<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">First Republic of Armenia</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1920 CE · capital Erivan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first modern Armenian state, born in 1918 amid war, genocide and famine, which survived two and a half years before Sovietisation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/transcaucasian-democratic-federative-republic">Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic</a> broke up, the Armenian National Council declared independence on 28 May 1918, just as Armenian forces halted an Ottoman offensive at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sardarabad">Sardarabad</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-batum">Treaty of Batum</a> in June 1918 left the new state a small territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Erivan</a>, crowded with refugees from the Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was governed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenian-revolutionary-federation">Armenian Revolutionary Federation</a>, with prime ministers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hovhannes-kajaznuni">Hovhannes Kajaznuni</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-khatisian">Alexander Khatisian</a> and a parliament elected by universal suffrage, including women. It faced famine, epidemics and border wars with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. After the Ottoman defeat it expanded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sevres</a> in 1920 promised it much of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In autumn 1920 Turkish nationalist forces invaded, and in December 1920 the government ceded power to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Bolsheviks</a>, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR</a>. Its flag and anthem were revived by independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> in 1990-1991.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/first-republic-of-armenia.jpg" alt="First Republic of Armenia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Stepan Malkhasyants Vector: Aivazovsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Armenia_(1918%E2%80%931922).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1920 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Erivan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hovhannes Kajaznuni, Alexander Khatisian, Simon Vratsian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Armenian Apostolic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Armenia, Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Armenia (1918-1920), Democratic Republic of Armenia</dd></dl></aside></div>
